Most people who regularly use AI already know the rule:

**Check the answer.**

Chatbots can hallucinate facts, misunderstand questions and confidently produce information that simply isn't true.

Yet knowing that and actually verifying an answer appear to be very different things.

Research from cybersecurity company F-Secure surveyed 1,500 AI users across the United States and United Kingdom and found a striking contradiction.

**89.5% said the responsibility and workload of checking chatbot answers falls on them.**

Yet F-Secure says **70% check AI responses only sometimes, rarely or never.**

The problem, then, isn't simply that people don't understand AI can make mistakes.

They understand.

They're just not consistently checking.

## Confidence is persuasive

One clue appears elsewhere in the research.

F-Secure found that **35.5% of users rarely or never check responses that sound confident.**

That's important because language models are remarkably good at sounding certain.

The tone of a response can feel authoritative even when the underlying information is questionable.

There may be no hesitation.

No visible uncertainty.

No obvious clue that a statement was generated from an incorrect assumption.

Humans naturally use confidence as one signal of credibility when communicating with other people.

AI inherits the appearance of that signal without necessarily possessing the knowledge that would justify it.

## The convenience problem

Verification also undermines one of AI's biggest advantages.

Speed.

If a chatbot summarizes a subject in ten seconds but you need another ten minutes to independently verify every claim, some of the convenience disappears.

For low-stakes questions, many users appear willing to accept that trade-off.

The problem emerges when the same behaviour carries over into more consequential areas.

F-Secure's research examined people using AI around topics including health, finances and relationships.

An incorrect restaurant recommendation is annoying.

Incorrect financial or medical information can have considerably more serious consequences.

The interface, however, may look exactly the same.

Same chatbot.

Same text box.

Same confident answer.

## We may be developing automation bias

There is a broader human behaviour at work here.

When a system repeatedly produces useful results, people naturally begin trusting it.

If your AI assistant answers 30 questions correctly, you're less likely to aggressively investigate answer number 31.

That is efficient most of the time.

But probabilistic AI systems make the occasional wrong answer particularly dangerous because the error can arrive wrapped in exactly the same polished language as everything else.

AI doesn't need to fool someone intentionally.

It only needs to be useful often enough that the person stops checking.

## Better AI needs better uncertainty

The obvious solution is "users should verify everything."

That's good advice.

It's also unrealistic.

AI systems are being integrated into search engines, operating systems, office software, phones and browsers precisely because they're supposed to remove friction.

If safe use requires users to independently research every response, we've transferred much of the work back to them.

A better long-term solution may involve AI becoming more transparent about uncertainty.

Systems could distinguish between:

information strongly supported by reliable sources,

reasonable inference,

and answers where confidence is genuinely low.

Sources and citations can help too, although citations themselves need to be checked.

## AI literacy is becoming ordinary literacy

For years, digital literacy meant understanding search engines, suspicious links, online privacy and whether a website could be trusted.

AI adds another skill:

knowing when **not** to trust a machine that sounds intelligent.

That may eventually become as basic as understanding that the first Google result isn't automatically true.

The difference is that search results usually show multiple sources.

A chatbot often collapses information into one clean, authoritative-sounding response.

That convenience changes how users evaluate information.

## TCF Journal Take

The most revealing part of F-Secure's research isn't that AI makes mistakes.

We already know that.

It's that humans are adapting to AI faster than our verification habits are adapting with it.

People don't need to believe a chatbot is perfect to become dependent on it.

They only need to believe it's probably right.

And as AI becomes more useful, that temptation becomes stronger.

The future of trustworthy AI therefore isn't only about building models that hallucinate less.

It's also about designing systems that help humans understand **when confidence is deserved**.

Until then, one habit remains surprisingly valuable:

When the answer actually matters, check it.
